  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1126 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nThursday forecast is very uncertain given unknown impacts from \nprevious day storms and high uncertainty regarding the position of \nthe frontal boundary and old outflow boundaries after the dust \nsettles. That said, Thursday appears to have more favorable \nparameters for an organized severe convective event than Wednesday \nas the ridge weakens, NW flow becomes better established, S/WVs look \nstronger, moisture axis remains good and for the first time in a \nlong time, notable wind shear is indicated by models. \nUnfortunately, confidence may not improve until we see the whites \nof its eye regarding the scope of Thursday's convective potential \nand this will bear watching to say the least. NBM shows most of \nthe area has a 15 to 40% chance of seeing 1/2 inch or more event \ntotal rainfall Wed-Thu and if anything this has slightly trended \nup the last few days.\n\nThe mid-upr level high center will be back on the move through the \nweekend and the center may reach the SE US by Monday, but models are \nnot in good agreement. In fact, the 12Z GEM was much slower with the \ntransition east and kept it centered over the area on Monday. In \ngeneral, above normal temperatures and isolated storms is forecast \nfor the weekend. The models continue to show a significant front \nwith associated POPs early next week but timing remains days apart. \nThe 6-10 day CPC outlook indicates a 50-60% chance for above normal \nprecip which suggests majority of the model ensembles are on board \nwith producing some beneficial rainfall with a frontal system.",
